								"__type" : "snippets",
								"items" : [ ]
							},
							"createEmail" : {
								"__type" : "email",
								"emailString" : "<unknown>"
							},
							"createTime" : {
								"__type" : "time",
								"time" : {
									"__type" : "dateAndTime",
									"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-11-18T00:06:25.592258+01:00"
								}
							},
							"editEmail" : {
								"__type" : "email",
								"emailString" : "<unknown>"
							},
							"editTime" : {
								"__type" : "time",
								"time" : {
									"__type" : "dateAndTime",
									"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-11-18T00:28:10.280926+01:00"
								}
							},
							"uid" : {
								"__type" : "uid",
								"uidString" : "+9oVwLP1SzOLS7WaglFhMA=="
							},
							"paragraphStyle" : {
								"__type" : "textStyle"
							},
							"string" : "In a running image almost everything reaches almost everything, through the UI, processes and announcers, so a tool's memory usually goes to whatever root reaches it first. Two questions help:\n- **What does it hold?** Add a focus group: it is estimated before all other groups and stops at the shared hubs of the image (processes, Bloc elements and spaces, the async thread pool), so that it gets its own objects, for example the history of the MCP server with its results.\n- **What does it keep alive?** A {{gtClass:GtMemorySpaceRetention}} splits the memory reached through the instances of some classes in two: what is reached **only through them** (retained, freed without them) and what is **also reached from other places** (shared, it would stay). Its Classes view shows the instances of each class and the memory retained through them. The retained part is measured in a single walk, so it does not depend on changes of the heap between walks."
						},
						{
							"__type" : "pharoSnippet",
							"children" : {
								"__type" : "snippets",
								"items" : [ ]
							},
							"createEmail" : {
								"__type" : "email",
